<!--ID=5B95B1CC-2C7B-494F-B746-CF22A0E779B7;Version=1|{"Locales":{"en-US":{"AutoApplyCustomLexiconFiles":[{}]},"en-GB":{"AutoApplyCustomLexiconFiles":[{}]}}}-->
<speak xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2001/10/synthesis"
       xmlns:mstts="http://www.w3.org/2001/mstts"
       xmlns:emo="http://www.w3.org/2009/10/emotionml"
       version="1.0"
       xml:lang="en-GB">
  <voice name="en-GB-OllieMultilingualNeural">
    <prosody rate="-11.00%" pitch="-2.60%">
            Psalm forty six speaks in the middle of upheaval, naming earth that gives way, mountains that move, and waters that roar and foam.<break time="500ms"/>
      Yet in the centre of that shaking world comes the command, Be still, and know that I am God.<break time="500ms"/>
      Stillness in this Psalm is not based on pretending the troubles are absent; it comes from knowing who God is in the middle of them.<break time="1000ms"/>
      The Lord of hosts is with us, the Psalm says. The God of Jacob is our fortress.<break time="500ms"/>
      So stillness is not vacancy; it is the soul settling under divine reality stronger than the shaking world.<break time="500ms"/>
      This Psalm allows rest to exist alongside turbulence because God's presence does not depend on outward calm first.<break time="1800ms"/>
      Nothing in this scene is outside God's rule.<break time="1000ms"/>
      Psalm forty six places stillness in the knowledge of God's rule and presence.<break time="1000ms"/>
      The command to be still rests on who God is, not on perfect conditions.<break time="1000ms"/>
      You can become quiet beneath the steadiness of God.<break time="1800ms"/>
      You can rest.<break time="3800ms"/>
    </prosody>
  </voice>
</speak>